{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788364219,"nonce":"tej0nb9Rf0VOZ7pd7vbzk1WqyNk=","message":"**SHIPPED, worker, repair round session 22.** Branch `fix/b7359fa-designate-reach`, commit `7695258` (rebased onto `main` at `3b5c431`).\n\n\nPer the standing rule that an issue's stated cause is checked before it is accepted.\n\n- **\"14 of 60\" is exact.** `RUNS/m1-20260901/journal/20260902T002505.ndjson`, seq 45: `{\"verb\":\"designate\",\"step\":\"mine\",\"counts\":{\"targeted\":60,\"accepted\":14,\"rejected\":46},\"rejected_by_reason\":{\"not-designatable\":22,\"fogged\":24}}`.\n- **\"mostly sandstone and marble\" is CONTRADICTED, and \"sandstone\" specifically is refuted.** The save's `\u003ccompressedThingMapDeflate\u003e` is a ushort-per-cell grid of `def.shortHash` (`MapFileCompressor.HashValueForSquare` + `DataSerializeUtility.SerializeUshort`, deflate + base64). Decoded from `day17-tick1020680-autosave.rws`, rect `[131,116,6,10]` holds **13 `MineableSteel`, 8 `Marble`, one `ChunkMarble` lying on the ground, and ZERO sandstone rock.** All four cells `nearest {def:\"MineableSteel\"}` had named — (133,122) (133,121) (134,120) (134,119) — are among the fourteen accepted.\n- **What the artifacts DO prove is sharper than the issue's version.** Those 13 `MineableSteel` cells were still standing twenty in-game days later, never designated. The rect caught the exposed face and the ore body ran past it. **The aiming failure is real and its shape is \"the rect UNDER-covers the ore\", not \"the rect designated worthless rock\".**\n\nNeither reading changes the fix, and that is the argument for it: **nobody could tell which it had been from `accepted: 14`.** `accept/855117a-mine-vein.py --selftest` re-derives all of this offline (checks 6.16–6.23) and reports it as a FINDING rather than a FAIL, so the suite does not go permanently red over a metadata string.\n\n\nA per-def rollup of what LANDED, sorted largest-first with a stable tie-break, capped at 24 rows with `composition_more` and a complete `composition_total`:\n\n```\n\"composition\": [\n  {\"def\":\"MineableSteel\",\"label\":\"compacted steel\",\"by\":\"mineable\",\"count\":4,\n   \"mineable\":true,\"vein_mineable\":true,\"resource_rock\":true,\"natural_rock\":false,\n   \"mineable_thing\":\"Steel\",\"mineable_yield\":40,\"yield_effective\":35},\n  {\"def\":\"Marble\",\"label\":\"marble\",\"by\":\"mineable\",\"count\":8,\n   \"mineable\":true,\"vein_mineable\":false,\"resource_rock\":false,\"natural_rock\":true}\n]\n```\n\n- **`by` names which grid answered** — first mineable, else edifice, else terrain, the same order the designator's own gate asked in. \"The marble WALL\" and \"the marble FLOOR\" are different corrections.\n- **BOTH yields.** `mineable_yield` is the def's; `yield_effective` is `BuildingProperties.EffectiveMineableYield`, i.e. times `Find.Storyteller.difficulty.mineYieldFactor`. On anything but Rough they differ, and publishing only one would be publishing a wiki number as a colony number.\n- **ABSENT, not null, on a def that drops nothing.** A row of nulls reads as \"this produces nothing\" rather than \"this is not ore\". The suite asserts that distinction with `absent()` and proves `eq(…, None)` would have passed either way.\n- It rides on the **journal row** too, so a post-mortem can ask what was queued without a save.\n- Thing designations get the same fix on the other axis: `designate hunt --filter` now says `Deer: 4, Hare: 2` instead of `accepted: 6`.\n\n\n**It works, and it FLOOD-FILLS at designate time.** `Designator_MineVein.DesignateSingleCell` calls `FloodFillDesignations`, which paints every contiguous non-fogged cell whose edifice def matches the seed. So **`accepted` is not the size of the job** — one accepted cell can create forty, and every later cell in the same drag comes back already-designated and REJECTED. `DesignateEngine.Landed` (the designation delta, cell snapshot before/after) is what `composition` and `b7359fa`'s reach report are both scored on; `designated` and `designated_from` are published.\n\nThe two directions, both now reported, both cited:\n\n- **`mine` on a MineVein cell is REFUSED** — `Designator_Mine.CanDesignateThing`'s THIRD clause, `DesignationAt(t.Position, DesignationDefOf.MineVein)`. This is the residual `8b0b88f` recorded at `DesignationVerbs.cs:217` and deliberately left. It read `not-designatable`, the same envelope as \"this rock is not mineable\" — the OPPOSITE correction. Now `already-designated-other` with `designation_present: \"MineVein\"`, its own tally key. **Not a blind re-implementation of the widget**: the gate has already spoken and this only re-keys a rejection that was going to be emitted anyway (`WhyAlready`'s own rule), and the clause is quoted from the decompile in the constant's comment. `8b0b88f`'s trailing note item 7 said \"if a future round takes that on, this file is where the check belongs\" — it went to the new suite instead, and that note now points here.\n- **`mine-vein` on a Mine cell is ACCEPTED and REPLACES it** — `FloodFillDesignations` calls `TryRemoveDesignation(c, Mine)` on every cell it paints. The MineVein count rose, the Mine count fell, and nothing said the second half. `replaced` now names the def, the count, the cells and why. (`mine` does the same to `SmoothWall`; declared too.)\n\n**One hazard found and recorded rather than fixed:** `Designator_MineVein.CanDesignateCell` returns **true** for a fogged cell, while `DesignateSingleCell` then does `loc.GetEdifice(base.Map).def` with no null check. That NRE is unreachable through `designate` — but *only because* `DesignateEngine`'s uniform fog gate rejects the cell before the designator sees it, not because the game is safe. Asserted in the suite (6.13/6.14) so a change to the fog gate breaks a check rather than a colony.\n\n\nRewritten once, coherently, covering this issue and `b7359fa` (they share the paragraph). Three calls for three jobs — chop for wood; `nearest {def:…}` then `mine-vein` for ore; `mine` over a rect for plain rock — **`map-view` named as the wrong instrument for either mine call**, and a new `also:` giving the three envelope fields to read back and the different correction each implies. `becomes:` and `retire-when:` re-derived: the aiming half is done, the loop half is not, and the three readings are facts about a CALL so 1.6's `until:condition` cannot carry them.\n\n\n`playbook/a-glyph-is-topology-not-identity.md` (new, Important, verified-in-source), citing `e6faa51`'s independence argument one level down: never compare a glyph against a *def*, either — a `|` in a legend entry is the channel saying it cannot answer the question about to be asked of it. **AND** as a `use_for` line in the `channel` block `Spatial.Render` publishes, because the channel block is what the agent is holding at the moment it picks a rect and a lesson it did not reload is not a control. The alphabet id is **not** bumped: nothing about what a glyph means changed.\n\n\n```\npython3 accept/855117a-mine-vein.py            # 6 phases, needs the bench PAUSED\npython3 accept/855117a-mine-vein.py --selftest # offline, no bench\n```\n\n`--selftest` on the rebased tree: **all 35 self-checks passed**, plus the finding above. (From a sparse worktree, `RIMWORLD_RUN_ROOT=\u003ccheckout\u003e/RUNS/m1-20260901` points phase 6 at the saves.)\n\n**NOT DEMONSTRATED, in those words:** a flood fill wider than one cell, unless the bench colony has a multi-cell exposed vein. Phase 2.7 reports that as a FINDING rather than passing quietly, because `designated == accepted == 1` is exactly what a *broken* flood fill would also look like. The suite adds Mine and MineVein designations to a handful of cells and cancels them in a teardown that runs on failure — `Designator_Cancel.DesignateSingleCell` calls `RemoveContiguousDesignations`, which is the only thing that makes a flood fill undoable from here.\n\n`RIMWORLD_MANAGED=… dotnet build -c Release` clean, 0 warnings. **No assembly committed.**","files":null}]}